.TH IO_GET_WANTWRITE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
io_get_wantwrite \- Check if an I/O entry wants to write

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/io_internal.h>

.IB int
.IB io_get_wantwrite(
.IB fd_type d
.IB );

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBio_get_wantwrite\fR function checks if an I/O entry associated with a file descriptor wants to write. It takes one parameter:

.IB fd_type d
The file descriptor for which to check if the I/O entry wants to write.

The function returns 1 if the I/O entry wants to write, or 0 otherwise.

The \fBio_get_wantwrite\fR function is intended to be used to determine if an I/O entry associated with a file descriptor is currently interested in writing data.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBio_get_wantwrite\fR function to check if an I/O entry wants to write:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/io_internal.h>

int main() {
    fd_type s = 123;  // Example file descriptor

    if (io_get_wantwrite(s)) {
        printf("I/O entry for fd %d wants to write\n", s);
    } else {
        printf("I/O entry for fd %d does not want to write\n", s);
    }

    return 0;
}
.nef